      <p>It happens at almost exactly the same time every evening. Someone's stomach growls, someone else opens the fridge and stares into it as if the answer is hiding behind the ketchup, and the question gets asked: <strong>"What do you want for dinner?"</strong> What follows is rarely a plan. It's usually a stalemate — "I don't mind," "whatever's fine," "you pick" — that somehow eats up half an hour and ends with everyone slightly annoyed and a pizza on the way.</p>

      <p>This isn't a willpower problem or a sign that your household can't communicate. It's a known cognitive pattern called <strong>decision fatigue</strong>: after a day spent making dozens of small choices — what to wear, which email to answer first, whether to take the call — your brain's appetite for one more open-ended decision is genuinely lower by evening. Dinner, which involves everyone's mood, dietary needs, and what's actually in the pantry, is exactly the kind of decision that fatigue hits hardest.</p>

      <p>The fix that's quietly caught on in a lot of households this year is almost embarrassingly simple: a <strong>free online spin wheel</strong>. Load it once with meals everyone's already agreed to, and let it make the call every night. No back-and-forth, no one person always being the default decider, no 30-minute negotiation before anyone's even turned the stove on.</p>

      <!-- Callout: New to spin wheels? -->
