          <div class="stat-val">$0</div>
          <div class="stat-label">Cost — no sign-up, no app download, works in any browser</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout blue">
        <div class="callout-icon">🧠</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          Curious why handing a decision to a random tool can feel like a relief rather than a loss of control? Our piece on <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making">the psychology behind random decision making</a> digs into why people often report being happier with an outcome they didn't personally agonize over.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Section 2 ── -->
      <h2 id="why-wheel-works">2. How a Spin Wheel Actually Fixes the Problem</h2>

      <p>A coin flip, a dice roll, and "let's just take turns" are all reasonable ways to make a random choice — so why does a spin wheel work better than any of them for dinner, specifically? A few reasons stand out once you actually use one for a few weeks:</p>

      <div class="idea-card">
        <div class="idea-card-head">
          <div class="idea-card-emoji">⚡</div>
          <div class="idea-card-meta">
            <div class="idea-card-label">Reason 01</div>
            <div class="idea-card-title">It scales past the limits of a coin or a dice</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="idea-card-body">A coin only ever gives you two outcomes. A dice caps out at six. Your actual dinner repertoire is probably closer to fifteen or twenty meals — and a spin wheel handles that without breaking a sweat, whether you load 8 entries or 40.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="idea-card">
        <div class="idea-card-head">
          <div class="idea-card-emoji">🎭</div>
          <div class="idea-card-meta">
            <div class="idea-card-label">Reason 02</div>
            <div class="idea-card-title">The spin itself defuses the tension</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="idea-card-body">There's a genuinely different feeling to watching a wheel slow down together than to reading a number off a random generator. Kids lean in. Someone calls out what they're hoping for. It turns a chore into a ten-second shared moment instead of one more silent app notification.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="idea-card">
        <div class="idea-card-head">
          <div class="idea-card-emoji">✅</div>
          <div class="idea-card-meta">
            <div class="idea-card-label">Reason 03</div>
            <div class="idea-card-title">Pre-loading is pre-agreeing — so nobody can veto the result</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="idea-card-body">Every entry on the wheel got there because someone in the household was fine eating it. So when it lands on "stir-fry again," there's genuinely nothing to argue about — everyone signed off on that outcome before the spin ever happened. That's the actual mechanism that ends the nightly debate: not randomness on its own, but randomness applied to a list you already agreed to.</div>
        <div class="idea-card-tip">💡 Try building the wheel together as a household activity. The conversation you have while adding entries does more to prevent future arguments than any rule you could set afterward.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="idea-card">
        <div class="idea-card-head">
          <div class="idea-card-emoji">♻️</div>
          <div class="idea-card-meta">
            <div class="idea-card-label">Reason 04</div>
            <div class="idea-card-title">Build it once, reuse it for months</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="idea-card-body">Bookmark the link and it's ready every evening in seconds — no re-typing your meal list from scratch. Prune dishes nobody's excited about anymore, add new ones as you discover them, and the wheel gets more accurate to your household's real tastes over time.</div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Section 3 ── -->
      <h2 id="setup">3. Building Your First Dinner Wheel in 5 Steps</h2>

      <p>You can have a working dinner wheel ready before the kettle boils. Here's the exact process:</p>

      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body"><strong>Open the tool on whatever device is nearby.</strong> Go to <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/">spinthewheelsonline.com</a> on your phone, tablet, or laptop — no account and nothing to install. It works the moment the page loads.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body"><strong>Add 10–14 meals your household actually likes.</strong> Mix quick weeknight staples with a couple of weekend-worthy options and one or two takeout fallbacks for the nights nobody wants to cook.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body"><strong>Save the link somewhere you'll actually find it.</strong> A home-screen bookmark or a pinned browser tab works best — the goal is zero friction between "I'm hungry" and "let's spin."</div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body"><strong>Agree on one rule before the first spin: the wheel is final.</strong> No re-spinning because someone's "not really feeling that tonight." This single rule is what keeps the wheel from turning back into a negotiation.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">5</div>
          <div class="step-body"><strong>Spin earlier than you think you need to.</strong> 5:00–5:30pm, before anyone's hungry enough to be short-tempered about the result, gives you time to actually prep whatever it picks.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Section 4 ── -->
      <h2 id="meal-ideas">4. 35 Dinner Ideas, Sorted by How Much Energy You Have</h2>

      <p>If your wheel feels empty, here's a starting list organized by effort rather than cuisine — because on a Tuesday, how much energy cooking will take often matters more than what kind of food it is. Swap freely for whatever your household actually eats; a generic list is just a starting point.</p>

