        <h2 id="faq">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How many meals should I put on my dinner wheel?</summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Somewhere between 10 and 16 works well for most households. Fewer than that and you'll cycle through the list too fast to feel like real variety; more than 25 and individual meals rarely get picked, which makes the wheel feel arbitrary rather than useful. Start with your most-cooked weeknight staples and adjust from there.</p>
            <p>For a full walkthrough of how the tool handles entries, see our <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool">complete guide to the Spin the Wheel tool</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the spin genuinely random?</summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Yes. Every spin computes an unbiased index using the browser's Web Cryptography API (<code>window.crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) with rejection sampling the moment you hit spin—so click timing cannot nudge or steer the landing dish.</p>
            <p>For the technical detail, see our article on <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random">whether a digital spin wheel is truly random</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if someone doesn't want what the wheel picks?</summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>This is exactly why the "wheel is final" rule matters, and why it's set <em>before</em> the first spin, not negotiated after. If everyone agreed to add a meal to the wheel, everyone agreed to eat it if it comes up. One meal that doesn't excite someone every so often is a much smaller cost than the negotiation it replaces.</p>
            <p>If a particular dish causes resistance more than once, that's useful feedback — take it off the wheel rather than arguing about it each time it lands.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can we use the wheel on a TV or shared screen at dinner time?</summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Yes — open it in a browser and cast to a smart TV or connected display, then spin in fullscreen for the full effect. It turns the daily decision into something the whole table watches together rather than a phone one person checks alone.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How is this different from a meal-planning app?</summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Meal-planning apps ask for upfront weekly effort — building a plan, a shopping list, a schedule. A dinner wheel flips that: set it up once, then spin each evening with almost no ongoing effort. It's less structured, but for a lot of households that's exactly why it's the one that actually gets used week after week, instead of the meal plan that gets abandoned by Thursday.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I use a wheel for other food-related decisions?</summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
